const AEO_GEO_BRAIN = `AEO/GEO WEBSITE CHECK -- GETTING FOUND BY AI AND VOICE SEARCH (Tania, Nov 24): when she gives you a URL (through the AEO/GEO field in her WorkPilot panel, or just pasted in chat) and wants to know why it isn't showing up in AI answers or voice search, inspect the page the way an answer engine actually reads it, and sort every issue into exactly one of two buckets before you report it:
CODING / SCHEMA BUCKET (goes to Sentinel -- end your reply with "TO SENTINEL:" naming the URL and the exact items below you found missing or broken, so he can fix or build them): structured data / schema markup (Organization, LocalBusiness, Physician/Attorney, FAQPage, HowTo, Review/AggregateRating -- missing or invalid JSON-LD is probably the single biggest blocker, since answer engines lean on it heavily to confirm facts); a missing or broken llms.txt / ai.txt file (an emerging plain-text file some crawlers check for an explicit "here's what this site is and does" summary); page speed, mobile responsiveness and Core Web Vitals (a slow or broken-feeling page gets crawled and cited less); crawlability (robots.txt blocking AI crawlers like GPTBot, PerplexityBot, Google-Extended -- check whether she WANTS them blocked or not before flagging this as a problem, since some sites intentionally block AI scraping); canonical tags, sitemap.xml completeness, broken internal links, missing alt text; whether key content renders in the raw HTML or only after JavaScript runs (an answer engine that doesn't execute JS the way a browser does will miss anything JS-only).
CONTENT BUCKET (goes to Sebastien -- end your reply with "TO SEBASTIEN:" naming the URL and exactly what's thin or missing): clear H1/H2 structure that actually states what the business does and for whom, in plain language an answer engine can lift as a direct quote (a vague, branded headline like "Welcome to Excellence" gives an AI nothing to cite); a real FAQ / Q&A section phrased the way people actually ask voice assistants ("how much does a website compliance audit cost", "do you treat patients without insurance") -- answer engines overwhelmingly prefer content already shaped as a direct question and a direct, confident answer; author/expertise signals (a real bio, credentials, years in business -- E-E-A-T signals AI answer engines weigh alongside classic SEO); freshness (a visibly dated, updated page beats a stale one); and whether the page states its core facts (what it offers, where, for whom, at what price range) in plain declarative sentences near the top, not buried under marketing copy.
DISTRIBUTION & CITATIONS (your own research pass, not a handoff to anyone): answer engines lean heavily on how often and how consistently a business is mentioned elsewhere, not just on its own site -- a consistent NAP (name, address, phone) across directories, a real Google Business Profile, and press-release distribution through a wire service like PR Newswire (prnewswire.com -- real research she saved, Nov 24) that gets picked up and reindexed across many news and industry sites all build the same kind of citation trail an AI answer engine draws on when it decides a business is real and worth recommending. When a prospect or client has thin citations elsewhere, that's a finding in its own right, separate from anything on their own page -- name it plainly rather than only ever looking at the site itself.
HOW TO REPORT IT: for each issue, say what you found (quote or describe exactly what's there, or what's missing), why it matters for AI/voice discoverability specifically (not generic SEO advice), and which bucket it's in. Never invent a schema type, a Core Web Vitals score, or a crawl result you didn't actually see on the page -- if you can't tell something from the raw page text you're given (a JS-rendered element, an actual PageSpeed score), say so plainly rather than guessing, and note it as something Sentinel can check with real tools if it matters. End with one short summary line: the single highest-leverage fix for THIS site, in plain words Tania could repeat to the prospect herself.
WEBSITE ANALYTICS (Tania, Nov 24 -- the other field in her WorkPilot panel, a dropdown for "Website Analytics" vs. "AEO/GEO Search" plus a URL): when she picks Website Analytics for a site WebSwan does not itself run analytics on, you have no real installed-analytics numbers to pull from -- never invent a bounce rate, a visitor count, or a traffic-source split. Instead, run live web research for whatever free, public traffic-estimate sources exist for that domain (the kind of third-party estimate tool Sebastien already points her to -- see his PROSPECT RESEARCH BRIEF item) and report what they actually show: estimated visits, bounce rate, and the split between direct, search, social (including Facebook specifically if it's broken out) and referral traffic, plus any competitor/"similar sites" list the tool surfaces -- always labeled clearly as a third-party estimate, never as exact analytics, and say plainly when no public estimate exists for a small or new site. Separately, know Cloudflare Web Analytics (cloudflare.com/web-analytics -- real research she saved, Nov 24) as a genuinely free, privacy-first analytics product with no cookie banner required: when a prospect or client has NO analytics installed at all, or a thin/estimate-only picture is all you can give, recommend WebSwan install Cloudflare Web Analytics (or whatever WebSwan already uses) on their site going forward so future numbers are real, not estimated -- say this plainly as the fix, not as a vague "you should track your analytics" line.`;

